1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:13,516
Ten thousand dollars of EURUSD, and ten thousand dollars of Bitcoin. Same size, same risk?

2
00:00:13,966 --> 00:00:21,045
Not at all. In our data, Bitcoin's risk is about seven times EURUSD's.

3
00:00:21,494 --> 00:00:27,372
Today: how much size each market gets in a portfolio, based on volatility.

4
00:00:28,372 --> 00:00:33,440
Hello, this is episode fifteen of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

5
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:40,578
Last time: portfolio. Spreading risk reduced the swings, but did not change the edge.

6
00:00:41,028 --> 00:00:44,737
Today, splitting the risk properly inside that portfolio.

7
00:00:45,737 --> 00:00:53,208
An easy way to measure volatility: ATR. How far a market usually moves in a day.

8
00:00:53,658 --> 00:01:04,263
Across our 42 markets, as a percent of price: EURUSD 0.59, gold 1.52.

9
00:01:04,714 --> 00:01:14,588
Nasdaq 1.58, and Bitcoin 4.12. The fastest, Solana, at 6.62.

10
00:01:15,588 --> 00:01:21,857
Our system puts the stop at 2 ATR. So on a ten thousand dollar position, the risk is:

11
00:01:22,308 --> 00:01:32,835
EURUSD 118 dollars, gold 305, Nasdaq 315, Bitcoin 824.

12
00:01:33,285 --> 00:01:42,113
Use equal size across 42 markets, and four crypto markets alone take 37.6 percent of the total risk.

13
00:01:43,113 --> 00:01:49,801
The fix: set size by risk, not by dollars. The same risk on every trade.

14
00:01:50,252 --> 00:01:58,140
Same formula as episode four: size equals risk dollars divided by the value of the stop distance.

15
00:01:58,591 --> 00:02:05,669
In a fast market the stop is far, so the size is small. In a slow market the stop is close, so the size is big.

16
00:02:06,669 --> 00:02:11,738
Account ten thousand, risk 1 percent, so 100 dollars.

17
00:02:12,188 --> 00:02:25,510
EURUSD at 1.096, stop 129 pips. 1,290 dollars per lot. Size 0.07 lot.

18
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:36,644
Gold at 3551, 10,800 per lot. The size comes out to 0.009, smaller than the minimum lot.

19
00:02:37,644 --> 00:02:43,600
This is a real problem for small accounts. The minimum lot itself breaks your fixed risk.

20
00:02:44,049 --> 00:02:50,006
Then two options: drop that market, or find an instrument with a smaller contract.

21
00:02:50,456 --> 00:02:54,009
But never force it in by raising the risk.

22
00:02:55,009 --> 00:03:02,400
Now the risk of the whole portfolio, called heat. In our backtest, up to 30 trades were open at once.

23
00:03:02,851 --> 00:03:09,198
At 1 percent each, that is 30 percent at stake at once. That is far too much.

24
00:03:09,649 --> 00:03:17,198
So cap total heat, say at 6 percent. When more trades are open, each one gets less risk.

25
00:03:18,198 --> 00:03:26,793
One more trap: markets that move together. Bitcoin and Ethereum have a daily correlation of 0.84.

26
00:03:27,243 --> 00:03:32,572
Four crypto trades open together are really one big bet.

27
00:03:33,022 --> 00:03:39,369
So treat markets that move together as one bucket, with its own limit.

28
00:03:40,369 --> 00:03:45,620
So, today in short. Equal dollars do not mean equal risk.

29
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:54,037
Set size by volatility, the same risk per trade. If the minimum lot breaks your risk, skip the market.

30
00:03:54,487 --> 00:03:59,738
Cap the total heat, and treat markets that move together as one bucket.

31
00:04:00,738 --> 00:04:09,489
Next episode: costs. Spread, slippage, commission. How a small expense eats the whole edge.

32
00:04:09,939 --> 00:04:14,772
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

33
00:04:15,222 --> 00:04:20,289
Our Telegram and Instagram are in the description. See you in the next episode.

